.TH CHTTPSVR_ROUTER_USE 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_router_use \- append middleware to a sub-router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_router_use(chttpsvr_router *router,
                                   chttpsvr_middleware_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends a middleware to the chain of the given sub-router.  It runs
after the global middleware and before the route handler, and only
for requests that the server routes through this sub-router.

It also runs for a request that was routed to this sub-router and
then rejected with 413 before its handler could run, and for the 404
or 405 of a path that this sub-router owns but that none of its
routes matches.  chttpsvr_subrouter(3) says which paths a sub-router
owns, and chttpsvr_use(3) gives the full rule and the rejections that
run no middleware at all.

The function is thread-safe and may be called at any time, even while
another thread runs chttpsvr_destroy on the owning server.  A call
that races such a destroy returns ccol_invalid_args and touches no
freed memory.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when router or fn is NULL, or when the server that
                  owns router has been destroyed.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
ccol_not_permitted when the middleware chain of this router is already
at the cap of 32 entries.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The NOTES section of chttpsvr_use covers three more points: the cap
that each router applies when you register a middleware, the limit
of 32 middleware steps per request in total when a request is
dispatched, and the rule that of two duplicate routes the first one
wins.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_use (3),
.BR chttpsvr_subrouter (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_set_context (3)
